Hours Worked: 10.5 hours

Work Completed:
Developed an alternative implementation of Section 36 for the MW–M31 merger analysis pipeline using a complementary methodology for halo morphology characterization. Replaced the original spherical harmonic and Cloud-In-Cell framework with a fabric tensor analysis based on orientation tensors and Woodcock shape parameters to quantify halo anisotropy, triaxiality, and principal-axis evolution across radial shells. Implemented eigenvalue decomposition routines, shell-wise orientation diagnostics, and temporal tracking of structural evolution throughout the merger sequence.

Designed and integrated an adaptive density-weighting framework using K-nearest-neighbor (KNN) density estimation together with Kernel Density Estimation (KDE) employing Scott's bandwidth selection. Constructed three-dimensional KDE density fields for halo structure analysis and implemented Minkowski functional calculations on the adaptive density field, including persistence-based topology diagnostics to identify long-lived morphological structures, characterize density peak evolution, and distinguish physical features from numerical noise.

Generated a comprehensive visualization suite including Woodcock parameter phase diagrams, fabric tensor heatmaps, density-weighted multipole comparisons, KDE-based Minkowski functional curves, persistence diagrams, morphology-plane trajectories, bandwidth sensitivity analyses, summary diagnostic panels, and time-evolution animations. Performed code refinement, documentation updates, configuration optimization, and validation to ensure compatibility with the overall MW–M31 merger analysis pipeline while providing an independent framework for cross-validation of the original morphological diagnostics.
